course: everyday
lesson: 390
title: Showing off
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Jake climbed onto the shed roof at the back of the playing field. «Watch this!» he yelled, looking at a group of girls by the fence, and jumped. He landed badly, twisted his ankle and spent the next two weeks on crutches. The worst part? The girls were busy with a skipping game and hadn't even seen. Jake felt brave for one second and silly for fourteen days.
explain: Showing off was everywhere that term. Maddie lifted the heaviest kit bag in PE to prove how strong she was, pulled a muscle in her back and missed the football match she'd waited a month for. Tom flicked his ruler at Lily to make his mates laugh; it caught the corner of her eye. She was fine after a cold compress, but Tom was sent to the head and his mates went very quiet. Ollie swore in front of Lily to look grown-up. Lily just looked unimpressed, and Mr Hughes, right behind him, heard every word.
explain: Jake pretended to start a fight with Sam to look tough in front of the Year Sixes. To Jake it was a joke. To Sam it wasn't: he was frightened and cried, and Jake felt awful and said sorry properly. Kieran rolled up his sleeve to show his scar from a street fight like it was a medal. Lily said: «That looks like it hurt a lot.» Kieran went quiet and admitted it had, and that he had been scared. A scar is not a trophy. Later Kieran raced a Station Road boy to the lamppost to show off, «loser buys crisps», and tripped flat on the pavement. The boy he was racing came back and helped him up.
explain: Mrs Okafor asked the class why people show off. After the giggles, they worked it out: people show off when they want to be liked or noticed. Not every show-off is bad. Maddie and Jake once had a monkey-bars contest, both fell off into the bark chips, both laughed and decided being friends was better than winning. So the trick is to like people as they are, so they don't need to show off, and to cheer kindness, not danger.
fact: People usually show off because they want to be liked, noticed or thought tough.
fact: Showing off with danger, like jumping off roofs, often ends in injuries.
fact: Lifting something too heavy can strain muscles, especially in the back.
fact: Flicking rulers or throwing things near faces can injure eyes.
fact: Swearing to look grown-up rarely impresses anyone and can get you into trouble.
fact: A pretend fight can really frighten the person it is aimed at.
fact: Scars from fights are reminders of pain and fear, not prizes.
fact: Friendly contests are fun when nobody gets hurt and nobody needs to win.
fact: Dares and cheering egg people on to take bigger risks.
fact: Liking someone as they are makes it less likely they will feel they must show off.
exercise: Why did Jake jump off the shed roof? | To impress the girls by the fence | Because a teacher told him to get up there | To fetch a ball stuck on the shed roof | Because he was being chased by a dog
exercise: What happened after Jake's jump? | He twisted his ankle, and the girls hadn't even seen | The girls cheered and clapped for him all the way home | He landed perfectly and won a bet | The shed roof fell in under him
exercise: What did Maddie's showing off in PE cost her? | A pulled muscle and the football match she wanted | A gold medal from the PE teacher | Nothing, she lifted it easily | A detention for lifting a bag
exercise: What is the hidden danger in flicking a ruler at someone? | It can hit and hurt their eye | It can break the ruler in half | It can make the teacher laugh at him | It can make the ruler go missing
exercise: How did Tom feel before the ruler hit Lily? | He thought he was being funny | He was angry with Lily about the game | He was trying to hurt her badly | He was bored of the long lesson
exercise: Ollie swore in front of Lily to look grown-up. How did Lily react? | She looked unimpressed | She thought he was very cool | She swore back at him in front of everyone | She asked him to teach her
exercise: Why did Jake's pretend fight with Sam go wrong? | Sam was really frightened and cried | Sam hit Jake first by mistake | The Year Sixes joined in for real and it got rough | Jake tripped and hurt his wrist
exercise: What did Jake do after Sam cried? | Felt awful and said sorry properly | Laughed about it and did it again the next day | Told Sam to toughen up | Blamed the Year Sixes
exercise: Kieran showed his scar like a medal. What did Lily say? | «That looks like it hurt a lot» | «Wow, you're so tough, I'm jealous» | «I've got a bigger one» | «Show me how you got it»
exercise: What did Kieran admit after Lily's words? | It had hurt and he had been scared | He had won the fight easily and wanted to show it | It was only a drawing | He wanted another scar
exercise: How did Maddie and Jake's monkey-bars contest end? | Both fell, laughed and chose friendship over winning | Jake won and Maddie sulked about it for a week | Maddie won the race and Jake would not speak to her | They both broke their arms
exercise: Why can a friendly contest be all right? | Nobody gets hurt and nobody needs to win | Because contests always end well | Because winning is all that matters | Because the teacher is never watching the playground
exercise: What did the class decide is the main reason people show off? | They want to be liked or noticed | They are bored of lessons | They want to get into trouble with the head | They have nothing else to do
exercise: Kieran raced a Station Road boy to show off and tripped. Who helped him up? | The boy he was racing | Nobody at all | A passing police officer | His older brother
exercise: Which is a braver thing than jumping off the shed roof? | Saying «no thanks, I'll just watch» when dared | Jumping off a higher roof next time to prove it | Jumping when nobody is looking | Daring someone else to jump instead
exercise: If people show off because they want to be liked, what helps most? | Liking them as they are | Laughing at them when they fail | Daring them to do bigger things | Ignoring them until they stop
test: «dare ya to jump off the garage roof, Jake did it». What's a sensible answer? | «No, Jake's still on crutches» | «Only if you film it» | «Fine, but from the low bit» | «If I land it, you owe me a quid»
test: Ollie: «watch this, I'll say a really bad word right in front of Miss». What's true? | It won't impress anyone worth impressing | It'll make everyone in the class respect him more | Teachers don't mind swearing | It's fine if he's quick about it
test: «look at my scar, got it in a scrap, sick or what». What's the honest response? | That must have really hurt | That's well cool, I want one | Who did you beat up | Show me how to get one
test: Jake: «it was only a joke fight, why's he crying?» What's true? | A pretend fight can really scare the other person | Sam's just a cry-baby who can't take a joke | Jokes can't make anyone scared | Sam should have fought back for real
test: Maddie: «bet I can carry the whole pile of mats on my own». Teacher: «Two people, please.» Why? | Too heavy a load can pull a muscle | The mats are worth a lot of money | Teachers like to be in charge | Maddie isn't allowed to touch mats
test: Kieran: «race ya to the lamppost, loser buys crisps». He trips, and the other boy helps him up. What did the race turn into? | A rivalry that ended in kindness | A fight that the streets will remember | A reason for a bigger race | A win for Kieran after all
test: Tom flicks his ruler at Lily's desk «for a laugh». You're sitting next to him. Best move? | Tell him to stop before he hits someone | Laugh so he does it again | Flick yours too, so you can join in | Say nothing, it's not your business to tell him
test: A new boy keeps boasting about his huge house and his fast bike. Using the lesson, what will most likely help? | Be friendly to him as he is | Tell him he's a liar | Boast about your house too | Leave him out until he stops
test: Monkey bars, Jake vs Maddie, crowd yelling «drop him, Maddie!». When is a contest like this OK? | When nobody gets hurt and nobody needs to win | When one of them falls over on purpose | When the crowd is loud enough to hear from the road | Only when there's a prize
test: Gran: «Why d'you reckon that boy keeps showing off at the park?» Which is the wisest guess? | He wants to be noticed and liked | He wants to be left alone | He is trying to get into trouble | He likes being hurt
exam: Identify the principal reason, as established in classroom discussion, for which children engage in showing off. | A desire to be liked or noticed by others | A wish to incur punishment | A desire to injure themselves deliberately | An absence of any motive whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a scar sustained in the course of a street altercation? | It is a reminder of pain and fear rather than a trophy | It is a mark of distinction to be displayed proudly to one's peers | It confers authority upon its bearer | It indicates that its bearer was victorious
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a simulated fight directed at a timid classmate for the amusement of onlookers. | It may cause genuine fear in the classmate and is unkind | It is harmless provided that no actual blow is struck | It is a recognised form of friendly play | It is acceptable when the onlookers are older pupils
exam: It is given that a pupil frequently engages in boastful and hazardous displays in order to gain the regard of his peers. State the response most likely to diminish such conduct. | The extension of friendly regard to the pupil as he is | The provision of dares of increasing difficulty | The public ridicule of the pupil's failures | The applause of each hazardous display
